1d:T480,**Step by step**:

1. Open the simulation with the button below. The GRAFCET is drawn in the project ("Séquence" program) but excluded from execution: only the Main's Ladder is simulated.
2. Start the simulation in **step-by-step** mode: each click on "Step one cycle" runs one scan, that is, one complete run of the program.
3. Open the watch table and add `X0` to `X3`, `cycle_start`, `end1`, `output1` and `output2` to it.
4. Hold `cycle_start` down (Space key, see the user manual, Help menu) and, without releasing it, click "Step one cycle" once, then release: the inputs are only read at the start of a scan, so `cycle_start` must be true at the moment of the click. Then advance scan by scan and note which `X` memories are true after each click.
5. Set `end1` to true (it is a simple input: it stays true), then advance scan by scan.
6. Go back to step `X0` (restart the simulation), set `end1` to true first, then hold `cycle_start` down during a click on "Step one cycle".

**Two questions**: when a transition is crossed, how many steps are active, and for how many scans? And when `cycle_start` and `end1` are true together, what happens?1e:T55f,<details><summary>Show the answers</summary>

**Observation 1**: when the transition is crossed, `X1` and `X2` are active together for one scan. Step 1 only turns off when it sees step 2 active, that is, one scan after step 2 was activated. During that scan, `output1` and `output2` are both commanded.

<div style="text-align:center"><figure><img src="/training/ladder/naive-overlap-chronogram-en.svg" width="520" alt="Timing diagram: end1 becomes true on scan 3, X2 activates right away but X1 only turns off on scan 4; X1 and X2, hence output1 and output2, are active together during scan 3" /></figure></div>

**Observation 2**: with `cycle_start` and `end1` true together from `X0`, three steps (`X0`, `X1` and `X2`) are active together for one scan, then only `X2` remains. This is the same overlap as in observation 1, over three steps: `output1` and `output2` are commanded together. The network of `X1` is written before that of `X2`: `X2` already reads the new value of `X1`, so two transitions are crossed in the same scan. That crossing is not the flaw, the overlap is: each step waits for the next one to turn it off.

This is not a simulation defect: a real PLC behaves the same way. It writes its physical outputs at the end of each scan, so the actuators really do receive both commands (for example both directions of a motor commanded together).

</details>1f:T43d,### The order of the networks

The networks run from top to bottom, each one seeing what the previous ones have just written. **Write the transitions in the order of the sequence**: `X0 -> X1`, then `X1 -> X2`, then `X2 -> X3`...

Consequence: if two consecutive transitions have their receptivity true at the same time, they are crossed on the same scan. This is the case of an "always true" transition (with no receptivity): the transition is crossed as soon as the step before it is active. On a machine, a scan is very short compared to the movement of a cylinder or a conveyor, so this has no visible effect on the outputs: the step passed through is deactivated in the very scan where it activates, before the outputs are computed. This is not the overlap of the previous exercise, where an output is commanded one scan too long. For the same reason, an edge or stored action carried by a step passed through this way would never run (see the step "Actions in Ladder: edge and numeric"). The limits of this rule are explained in module A5, along with the method that avoids them.20:T40d,### Before the transition networks: start on the initial step

Without an active initial step, nothing starts. At power-up no step is active: a network must activate `X0`. Three arrangements exist.

- **No step active (reference)**: a chain of NC contacts, one per step, followed by an S coil on `X0`. If no step is active, `X0` is activated. No memory is needed, and the arrangement also recovers the GRAFCET if it ever ends up with no active step. With several initial steps, put one S coil in parallel per initial step.
- **`init` memory**: `init` is false at start-up. Network 1 activates the initial step, network 2 latches `init`, and network 1 no longer triggers.
- **System bit `_SYS_FIRST_SCAN`**: Studomate provides this variable, true on the first scan only. A contact on this bit replaces the `init` memory and its two networks. PLC vendors have the equivalent: the first-scan bit `FirstScan` at Siemens, `S:FS` at Rockwell, `%S13` at Schneider (three PLC manufacturers).

Place these networks before the transition networks.21:T486,<details><summary>Show the answers</summary>

A step memory is set to 1 by an S coil when the transition before it is crossed, and set back to 0 by an R coil when the transition after it is crossed. The network of a transition puts the upstream step and the receptivity in series, then does in parallel the reset of the upstream step and the set of the downstream step; the networks are written in the order of the sequence, after the activation of the initial step. `Automatic_cycle` contains the evolution (initial step and transitions), `Outputs` one network per output; the Main calls `Automatic_cycle` then `Outputs`, so that the outputs read steps that have finished evolving. A continuous action follows the step; a stored action stays true until a Reset (S and R coils); an edge action runs once (P or N contact on the step memory). The TON has the step as its input and goes in the network of the transition it delays; to delay an output, you add a step and a timed transition. A fault is latched in `Faults`, called first: the acknowledgement network (R) is written before the latching one (S), which wins as long as the cause persists.

</details>7:["$","$L17",null,{"maxWidth":"lg","sx":{"my":4},"children":["$L18",["$","$L19",null,{"variant":"h2","component":"p","color":"primary","gutterBottom":true,"children":"Module A4: From GRAFCET to Ladder (part 1)"}],false,["$","$L1a",null,{"sx":{"my":2}}],["$","$L1b",null,{"children":[false,["$","$L1c",null,{"steps":[{"id":"theory-steps-ladder","kind":"theory","title":"A step is a memory","body":["### Before you start\n\nThis module reuses notions from the previous modules (go back to the module mentioned if one of them escapes you):\n\n- **GRAFCET (A1)**: a **step** is a square, active or inactive; a **transition** links two steps and carries a **receptivity**, the condition to go from one to the other; a **continuous action** runs while its step is active (other execution modes exist, see module A1).\n- **S and R coils (A2)**: S (set) sets a boolean variable to 1 and it stays there, R (reset) sets it back to 0.\n- **P contact (A2)**: conducting for a single scan, at the instant the variable goes from false to true.\n- **TON timer (A3)**: its output Q becomes true when the time PT has elapsed.\n- **Scan (A0)**: one complete run of the PLC program, repeated in a loop.\n- **Subprograms (A3)**: a separate program, called by the Main with a call block, in the order the calls are written.","### An active step is a bit set to 1\n\nTake an oven: the \"heat\" step turns on when the start button is pressed, and turns off when the temperature is reached. In Ladder, each step becomes a **memory**: a boolean variable `Xn`, at 1 while the step is active. It behaves as follows:\n\n1. **It becomes active** when the transition just before it is crossed: the previous step is active AND its receptivity is true. An **S** coil sets `Xn` to 1.\n2. **It stays active** by itself: the S coil keeps the memory at 1, nothing needs to call it again.\n3. **It turns off** when the transition just after it is crossed. An **R** coil sets `Xn` back to 0.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/step-memory-states-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"Three moments of a step n: at rest, Xn-1 is active but Rn-1 is false and Xn stays 0; when Rn-1 becomes true, the transition is crossed, Xn-1 is set back to 0 by an R coil and Xn becomes 1 through an S coil; afterwards Xn stays 1 as long as Rn is false\" /></figure></div>","### Key points\n\n- A step is a memory `Xn`, at 1 when the step is active.\n- It turns on with an S coil when the transition before it is crossed, and turns off with an R coil when the transition after it is crossed.\n- The step \"One network per transition\" shows how to write these two coils in a single network.","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\nThe GRAFCET **evolution rules** are set by the IEC 60848 standard. According to rule 4, several transitions that can be crossed at the same time are crossed at the same time. A network per transition applies this principle to one transition: it deactivates the upstream step and activates the downstream step together. What changes when several transitions follow one another is explained in module A5.\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"exercise-grafcet-naive","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: diagnose the next-step method","body":["You know how to write the network of a transition. Before seeing how to assemble them in the right order, here is a way of implementing a GRAFCET that is often met, and its flaws. Here is the GRAFCET of the linear sequence from module A1.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/exercise-linear-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"280\" alt=\"GRAFCET of the linear sequence: initial step 0, transition cycle_start, step 1 driving output1, transition end1, step 2 driving output2, transition end2, step 3 with no action, then an always-true transition back to step 0\" /></figure></div>","It is already implemented in Ladder with the so-called \"next-step\" method: each step has its own self-holding circuit, activated by the previous step and deactivated by **the next step** (`Xn = Xn-1 AND Rn-1 + Xn AND NOT Xn+1`). There is no program to write: you observe, then you explain.\n\n**Inputs**: `cycle_start` (push button), `end1` and `end2` (limit switches). **Outputs**: `output1` and `output2`. The step memories are `X0` to `X3`.","$1d","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. Look at `X1` and `X2` on the scan where `end1` becomes true: which one turns off first?\n2. Step 1 deactivates thanks to step 2: on which scan does `X2` become true, and on which scan does `X1` see it?\n3. In which order are the networks `X0`, `X1`, `X2`, `X3` written? Which network reads the value computed by which other?\n\n</details>","$1e","The method of the step \"A network per transition\", seen next, removes the overlap: a network per transition turns off the upstream step and turns on the downstream step in the same scan. Several transitions can still be crossed in one scan, but when the networks are written in the order of the sequence, it always happens the same way."],"embeddedTemplateId":"linear-sequence-naive-ladder","embeddedWatchOpen":true,"embeddedInitialView":{"type":"ladder"},"cta":{"templateId":"linear-sequence-naive-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Watch the simulation","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"theory-method-ladder","kind":"theory","title":"One network per transition","body":["### From GRAFCET to Ladder, transition by transition\n\nTo implement a GRAFCET, you first write **the initial step**, then **one network per transition**, in the order of the sequence. This step details these two parts; the next step arranges the networks into two programs and adds the outputs.\n\nIn this module, \"reset\" means the R coil of a step memory. \"Reinitialise\" is reserved for the whole GRAFCET (module A5).","### One network per transition\n\nEach transition is written as **a single Ladder network**. From left to right: the step before the transition (one contact), then its receptivity (contacts in series), then, **in parallel**, the R coil of the step before and the S coil of the step after. When the network is true, the transition is crossed: one step turns off and the other turns on in the same scan.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/transition-network-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Ladder network of the transition between steps 1 and 2: the contact X1 in series with the contact R1, then in parallel the R coil of X1 and the S coil of X2\" /></figure></div>","Name the network after its transition, for example `X1 -> X2`, optionally followed by a description: the network title lets you find each transition of the GRAFCET at a glance.","**Check question**: \n\n`Xn-1 = 1`, `Rn-1 = 1`, `Xn = 0`: what are `Xn` and `Xn-1` after the network `Xn-1 -> Xn`?\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\n`Xn` is 1 and `Xn-1` is 0. Crossing the transition between them activates step n and deactivates step n-1.\n\n\n\n</details>","$1f","$20","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/initial-step-methods-en.svg\" width=\"480\" alt=\"Three initialisation arrangements: as the reference, NC contacts X0, X1, X2 and so on, one per step, followed by an S coil on X0; as a variant, an NC contact init that activates X0 then a second network that latches init; as a variant, a contact on the system bit _SYS_FIRST_SCAN that activates X0\" /></figure></div>","### Key points\n\n- A transition is a network: the upstream step and the receptivity in series, then in parallel a reset of the upstream step and a set of the downstream step.\n- The networks are written in the order of the sequence, after the activation network of the initial step.\n- These networks only contain the R and S coils of the step memories: the outputs are written elsewhere (next step)."]},{"id":"theory-two-programs","kind":"theory","title":"Two programs: Automatic_cycle and Outputs","body":["### Separating evolution and outputs\n\nSo far, all the networks are in a single program. With the outputs, a GRAFCET quickly has dozens of networks: they are arranged into **two programs**, called by the Main (module A3):\n\n1. **`Automatic_cycle`**: the evolution of the GRAFCET, that is the initial step, then one network per transition (and, later, the reset);\n2. **`Outputs`**: one network per output, which reads the step memories.\n\nThe Main contains two networks, in this order: the call of `Automatic_cycle`, then the call of `Outputs`. Calls run from top to bottom: the step memories have therefore finished evolving when the outputs are computed, without having to think about it. In the exercises, the Main also calls the operative part (the simulated machine) first: do not modify it.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/transition-method-en.svg\" width=\"300\" alt=\"The Main first calls the Automatic_cycle program (initial step, one network per transition in order), then the Outputs program (one network per output)\" /></figure></div>","### A complete example\n\nHere is a linear GRAFCET with three steps (transitions `a`, `b` and `c`, outputs `s1` and `s2`), with its networks, arranged in the two programs. A transition that starts from several steps (AND convergence) puts all those steps in series: it is covered with the divergences.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/transition-example-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"On the left, a linear GRAFCET with three steps (transitions a, b and c, s1 at step 1, s2 at step 2); on the right, its Ladder networks: the initial step activation (NC contacts X0 to X2 in series, then set of X0), X0 -> X1 (contacts X0 and a, then in parallel reset of X0 and set of X1), X1 -> X2 (X1 and b), X2 -> X0 (X2 and c), then s1 driven by X1 and s2 by X2\" /></figure></div>","**Check question**: \n\nWhy are the outputs in a program called after `Automatic_cycle`, rather than before?\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\nThe networks of a program see what the previous ones have just written. Called after `Automatic_cycle`, `Outputs` reads step memories that have finished evolving in this scan: the outputs follow the state of the steps of the same scan. Called before, it would read the previous scan's state, and the outputs would be one scan late.\n\n\n\n</details>","### Checklist\n\nBefore simulating, check:\n\n- one network per transition, named after its steps (`X0 -> X1`)?\n- the upstream step and the receptivity in series, then a reset of the upstream step and a set of the downstream step in parallel?\n- if the receptivity contains a delay, the TON right after the step contact, before the other contacts?\n- the networks written in the order of the sequence?\n- the step memories written by S and R coils, never by a plain coil?\n- the initial step activated only once, at start-up?\n- the transitions in `Automatic_cycle`, the outputs in `Outputs`, one network per output?\n- in the Main, the call of `Automatic_cycle`, then the call of `Outputs`?","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\n- **Transient evolution**: the IEC 60848 standard allows several cascading crossings in the same instant, until the situation is stable. With this method, the cascade depends on the order in which the networks are written.\n- **Edge receptivity** (↑a, true at the instant `a` goes from false to true): it is implemented with a P contact in the network of the transition.\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"exercise-grafcet-linear","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: implement a linear sequence","body":["First programming exercise of the module. Implement in Ladder the GRAFCET of the linear sequence from module A1, with one network per transition, arranged in the `Automatic_cycle` and `Outputs` programs, already created and called by the Main. You saw the flaws of the next-step method in the previous exercise: here, the effort is on the method.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/exercise-linear-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"280\" alt=\"GRAFCET to implement: initial step 0, transition cycle_start, step 1 driving output1, transition end1, step 2 driving output2, transition end2, step 3 with no action, then an always-true transition back to step 0\" /></figure></div>","**Provided variables**: inputs `cycle_start` (push button), `end1` and `end2` (limit switches); outputs `output1` and `output2`; memories `X0` to `X3` (one per step). The actions are **continuous**: an output is true while its step is active.\n\n**Work to do**:\n\n1. In `Automatic_cycle`, activate `X0` at start-up, just once.\n2. In `Automatic_cycle`, write one network per transition (`X0 -> X1` to `X3 -> X0`), in the order of the sequence.\n3. In `Outputs`, write one network per output.","**How to tell it is right**: in simulation,\n\n- at start-up, only `X0` is true;\n- on `cycle_start`, only `X1` is true and `output1` turns on;\n- on `end1`, only `X2` is true: `output1` turns off and `output2` turns on, never two steps active together;\n- on `end2`, `X0` becomes true again: step 3, with no action, is activated then deactivated on the same scan since the receptivity of its transition is always true, and the sequence is ready to start again;\n- the analysis panel reports no error.\n\nOrder of magnitude: `Automatic_cycle` has 2 initialisation networks and 4 transition networks, `Outputs` has 2 output networks, 8 networks in all.","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. Start with one transition: write `X0 -> X1` with the contact `X0`, the contact `cycle_start`, then the two coils in parallel. How many contacts for `X3 -> X0`, which has no receptivity?\n2. In which order should the transition networks be written? Written in the opposite order (`X1 -> X2` before `X0 -> X1`), the networks cross only one transition per scan, but each step stays visible one scan longer: it is not wrong, it is one scan of delay per step. The order of the sequence avoids this delay.\n3. Don't forget the initial step: without it, no step is active and the sequence never starts.\n\n</details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"linear-sequence-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"theory-actions-ladder","kind":"theory","title":"Actions in Ladder: continuous and stored","body":["The actions of a GRAFCET are all written in the **`Outputs`** program, one network per output. Each execution mode seen in module A1 has its Ladder equivalent. This step covers the actions that follow the step; the next step covers those that run just once.\n\n- **Continuous action** (\"Continuous\" mode): true while the step is active. A contact on the step memory and a coil: `Xn` then `(output)`. If several steps command the same output, their contacts are in parallel (OR).\n- **Stored action** (\"Set\" and \"Reset\" modes): the output stays true after the step ends, until a Reset. An S coil on the step that starts it, an R coil on the step that stops it.\n- **Conditional action**: executed if the step is active AND a condition is true. Studomate's GRAFCET editor does not offer it (an action carries no condition of its own), but in Ladder it is written with two contacts in series: `Xn` and the condition.","**Check question**: \n\nAn output `motor` is started by step 1, stopped by step 4, and must stay true in between. Which coils are needed, and on which steps?\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\nAn S coil on `motor` in a network that reads `X1`, and an R coil on `motor` in a network that reads `X4`, both in `Outputs`. A continuous action would not do: `motor` would drop as soon as step 1 ends.\n\n\n\n</details>","### Key points\n\n- Actions are written in `Outputs`, never in the transition networks.\n- Continuous: `Xn` then the coil, the steps that command the same output in parallel (OR). Stored: S on the step that starts it, R on the one that stops it.\n- Conditional: `Xn` AND the condition, in series."]},{"id":"theory-edge-actions-ladder","kind":"theory","title":"Actions in Ladder: edge and numeric","body":["An **edge action** (\"Rising edge\" and \"Falling edge\" modes) is executed just once, when the step activates or deactivates (what the standard calls an action on activation and on deactivation). In `Outputs`, a P contact (activation) or N contact (deactivation) on the step memory, followed by the action. A **numeric** action (an assignment, a calculation) is almost always an edge action: executed on every scan, it would be repeated.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/actions-types-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"Three-column table: a continuous action motor on step 2 becomes a contact X2 and a coil motor; the Set and Reset motor actions of steps 1 and 4 become an S coil on X1 and an R coil on X4; an action `spaces := spaces + 1` on rising edge becomes a P contact on X3 followed by a Calc block; the same action on falling edge uses an N contact\" /></figure></div>","### Example: counting cars\n\nThe numeric assignment `spaces := spaces + 1` (\"Rising edge\" mode) counts a car as it enters: a P contact on the step, followed by a Calc block. Without the P contact, the action would run on **every scan** while the step is active: this is the increment trap seen in module A3 (with a 100 ms scan, `spaces` would grow by 10 every second spent in the step).\n\nThese networks are in `Outputs`: `Automatic_cycle` only contains the R and S coils of the step memories.\n\n> **Warning**: a step followed by an always-true transition is activated and then deactivated in `Automatic_cycle`, in the same scan; when `Outputs` runs, it is already 0. A P contact, an S or R coil carried by this step therefore never runs, whereas the IEC 60848 standard does execute these actions. Avoid putting such an action behind an always-true transition, or use the three-block method (module A5, steps \"Going further\").","**Check question**: \n\nStep 3 stays active for 4 scans, with the action `spaces := spaces + 1`. By how much does `spaces` increase with a P contact on `X3`, then without?\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\nby 1 with the P contact (a single scan, when the step activates), by 4 without (once per scan).\n\n\n\n</details>","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\nThe IEC 60848 standard defines other action types than those of the Studomate editor (conditional, delayed, time-limited, on event). The *on event* action is different from the step's edge action: it is triggered by an input event (for example `↑a`) while the step is active, not by the activation of the step itself. Time is the subject of the next two steps.\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"theory-receptivity-timer","kind":"theory","title":"Time: a delay in a receptivity","body":["Reminder (module A3): a **TON** is a timer. Its input `IN` starts the count, its output `Q` becomes true when the time `PT` has elapsed, and drops when `IN` drops. In a GRAFCET, `IN` is the step: the time counts while the step is active. In this method, a TON only serves to cross a transition after a delay: it goes in `Automatic_cycle`, never in `Outputs`.","### A delay in a receptivity\n\nExample: a traffic light stays green for 5 s. In the GRAFCET editor (module A1), the receptivity is written `t1/X10/5s`. In Ladder, the TON (for example `t_green`, `PT = T#5s`) goes **inside the network of the transition**, right after the step contact and before the other contacts of the receptivity: `Xn`, the TON, the contacts of the receptivity, then the reset of `Xn` and the set of the next step. Its input `IN` is thus the step alone: time counts while the step is active. Another contact placed before the TON would make it count only while that contact is true. When the receptivity contains only the delay, what remains is `Xn` then the TON. If the step has several exit transitions, the TON is in one of them; the others read its output with the contact `t_green.Q`.","### Delaying or limiting an output\n\nTo make an output start only after a delay, or last only a given time, you do not put a TON in `Outputs`: you add a **step** to the GRAFCET, with a timed transition (`t1/X10/5s`, module A1). Example: a fan that starts 5 s after heating begins is driven by a \"ventilation\" step, reached by a timed transition; a lamp lit for only 5 s is driven by a step that the same delay leaves. Time thus stays in the transitions, where the GRAFCET expresses it.","**Check question**: \n\nIn which program does the TON of a receptivity delay go, and why is its input `IN` the step memory alone?\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\nIn `Automatic_cycle`, in the network of the transition it delays, right after the step contact. With `IN` = the step alone, time counts while the step is active and restarts from zero at each new activation; another contact placed before the TON would make it count only while that contact is true.\n\n\n\n</details>","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\nThe IEC 60848 standard writes a delay in a receptivity `5s/X10`; the old French notation (NF C 03-190, a French standard) writes `T/X10/5s` and is still present in many textbooks. Studomate's syntax (`t1/X10/5s`) is close to the latter. The standard also defines delayed and time-limited actions, which Studomate does not offer: the same effect is obtained with a step and a timed transition.\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"theory-movement-supervision","kind":"theory","title":"Time: supervising a movement","body":["A real machine supervises its movements: if a cylinder does not reach its limit switch in time, something is stuck. A **fault** is a variable (for example a memory set to 1, which can light an indicator) that signals this problem. Announced in module A3, the supervision uses a TON on the movement step, whose `Q` becomes true if the receptivity (the end-of-movement sensor) has not arrived in time. Then `Q` crosses a **fallback transition**, which leaves the step, and a coil latches the fault.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/movement-supervision-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Movement supervision: on the left, step 1 has two exit transitions, b to step 2 and t_watchdog.Q to step 3; on the right, in Automatic_cycle contact X1 starts the 8 s TON t_watchdog, and in Faults contact t_watchdog.Q sets the fault with an S coil\" /></figure></div>","### A third program: `Faults`\n\nThe TON is in the network of the fallback transition, therefore in `Automatic_cycle`. The fault itself is written in a third program, **`Faults`**, which the Main calls **first**, before `Automatic_cycle` then `Outputs` (after the operative part, in the exercises). The fault bits are thus set at the start of the scan and available for everything that follows: the evolution of the steps as well as the outputs.\n\n`Faults` reads `t_watchdog.Q` one scan late (the value of the previous scan), since the TON is computed further down in the scan. For a fault that does not need to react in the very same scan, this changes nothing.","### Acknowledging the fault\n\n`Faults` contains two networks, written in this order:\n\n1. **the acknowledgement**: the `acknowledge` push button sets the fault back to 0 (R coil);\n2. **the latching**: `t_watchdog.Q` sets the fault to 1 (S coil).\n\nThe latching network is written second so that it wins: if the cause of the fault persists (a stuck sensor, for example), the fault is set again as long as `t_watchdog.Q` is true, even if the operator presses `acknowledge`. An acknowledgement must not clear a fault whose cause is still there.\n\nTo forbid restarting until the fault is acknowledged, the receptivity of the transition that leaves the initial step also reads the fault: `cycle_start AND NOT fault`.","**Check question**: \n\nWhy is the network that latches the fault written after the acknowledgement network?\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\nThe last network that writes a variable wins. Placed second, the S coil wins over the R coil when the acknowledgement and the cause of the fault are true together: the fault stays set as long as its cause is present.\n\n\n\n</details>","### Summary\n\n| Case | Example | What changes in the Ladder |\n|---|---|---|\n| A delay in a receptivity | the light stays green for 5 s | the TON is in the network of the transition (`Automatic_cycle`), after the step contact: `Xn`, TON, `Rn` |\n| Movement supervision | fault if the drill does not reach the bottom in 8 s | `Q` crosses a fallback transition (`Automatic_cycle`) and sets the fault to 1 (S coil, in `Faults`) |\n\nIn both cases, the TON has the step memory as its input `IN`."]},{"id":"synthesis-drilling","kind":"synthesis","title":"Synthesis: drilling station","body":["A drilling station: the **operative part** (the machine: drill, motor, sensors) is provided; you write the **control part** (the PLC program that drives it). The project already contains the operative part, called by the Main: do not modify it. You create the `Faults`, `Automatic_cycle` and `Outputs` programs and call them from the Main, in this order, after the operative part.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/drilling-station-en.svg\" width=\"540\" alt=\"Side view of a column drill with its sensors h (drill up) and b (drill down), and a table of the variables: inputs cycle_start, h, b, blocked and acknowledge, outputs down, up and spindle, memory fault\" /></figure></div>","**Specification**:\n\n1. On `cycle_start`, the drill goes down (`down` and `spindle`) until sensor `b`. `cycle_start` is only taken into account if `fault` is false (`cycle_start AND NOT fault`).\n2. It drills for 2 s: `spindle` stays true, the delay is a TON.\n3. It goes back up (`up` and `spindle`) until sensor `h`, then the station waits for a new press of `cycle_start`.\n4. **Descent supervision**: if the drill does not reach `b` within 8 s (a second TON), the drill goes straight back up and `Faults` latches a fault in the variable `fault` (already created in the project).\n5. **Acknowledgement**: a press on the `acknowledge` push button clears the fault. As long as the fault is not acknowledged, a press on `cycle_start` does not start a cycle.\n\nTo test the supervision, use the `blocked` switch: while it is true, the drill no longer advances downwards.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/drilling-supervision-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"GRAFCET of the station: initial step 0, cycle_start AND NOT fault, step 1 (down, spindle), then b to step 2 (spindle) and t_drill.Q to step 3 (up, spindle), then h to step 0; a fallback branch t_watchdog.Q links step 1 directly to step 3 if the drill does not reach the bottom within 8 s\" /></figure></div>","The names of the two TONs (`t_drill`: `IN` = step 2, 2 s; `t_watchdog`: `IN` = step 1, 8 s) are those of the figure: you choose your own.","**How to tell it is right**: in simulation,\n\n- at rest, only `X0` is true;\n- on `cycle_start`, `down` and `spindle` are true until `b`;\n- at `b`, `down` drops, `spindle` stays true for 2 s, then `up` turns on until `h`;\n- with `blocked` true, after 8 s `fault` becomes true and `up` turns on: the drill goes back up;\n- `fault` stays true, and a press on `cycle_start` does not start a cycle, until a press on `acknowledge`;\n- after the acknowledgement, `cycle_start` starts a cycle again;\n- the analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. Three programs in the Main, in this order: `Faults`, `Automatic_cycle`, `Outputs`. Combine the steps \"Actions in Ladder\" and \"Time\": one TON for the drilling, another for the supervision, each in the network of its transition.\n2. The supervision is one more transition leaving the descent step, `t_watchdog.Q`. With the transition method it is handled like the others: a network `X1 -> X3`, with the TON `t_watchdog` right after the contact `X1`, then the reset of `X1` and the set of `X3`.\n3. In `Faults`, the acknowledgement network (R coil) is written before the latching one (S coil, on `t_watchdog.Q`). Why this order?\n4. The cycle start `X0 -> X1` reads `cycle_start` and the opposite of `fault` (NC contact).\n\n</details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"drilling-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"conclusion","kind":"theory","title":"Conclusion","body":["You can now implement a GRAFCET without divergences in Ladder, from the step to the outputs. Take stock, notion by notion:\n\n- **step and memory**: how does a memory `Xn` turn on and turn off?\n- **transition**: what does the network of a transition contain, and in which order should the networks be written?\n- **two programs**: what do `Automatic_cycle` and `Outputs` contain, and in which order does the Main call them?\n- **actions**: what is the difference between a continuous, stored and edge action? Where does a P contact go?\n- **time**: where does the TON of a receptivity delay go? How do you delay an output?\n- **faults**: where is a fault latched, and why is the latching network written after the acknowledgement?","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/transition-method-en.svg\" width=\"300\" alt=\"Cheat sheet: the Main calls Automatic_cycle (initial step, one network per transition in the order of the sequence), then Outputs (one network per output)\" /></figure></div>","$21","Next: module A5 covers OR and AND divergences, stopping a sequence and the limits of one network per transition. To check yourself, redo the drilling station challenge without help."]}],"prevLabel":"Previous","nextLabel":"Next","moduleId":"a4","initialStepId":"synthesis-drilling","nextModule":{"href":"/training/a5","label":"Move on to module A5"}}]]}],"$L22","$L23"]}]
